  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Showers and scattered thunderstorms late this \nafternoon through Sunday afternoon.\n\nA deepening area of low pressure, advancing from Michigan this \nmorning to just north of Lakes Erie and Ontario Sunday morning-\nafternoon will push a warm front across our region today, followed \nby a cold front late tonight through midday Sunday. \n\nInstability will increase to around 500 to 1000 J/KG of MUCAPE late \nthis afternoon and through the evening hours which will support \nscattered thunderstorms. A modest wind flow through 10K feet\ncould bring a few stronger storms with damaging wind gusts,\nthough the overall warm, moist environment with a 10K warm \ncloud depth is not the greatest for large hail growth. Combined\nwith peak heating of the day, a few stronger storms will exist \nacross our region (marginal to slight risk level west of the \nGenesee Valley), before overnight convection and instability \nbegins to wane. Any storm in this increasingly moisture rich \nenvironment could bring a locally heavy downpour. Additionally, \nas increasing EL heights force the convective cloud depth to \ngrow to over 25K feet, combined with a deepening 850 hPa to lake\nsurface temperature gradient, a few waterspouts may form under \nthe approaching upper level low. \n\nA shortwave trough within the base of the negatively tilted upper\nlevel low early Sunday combined with the occluded front will \npass across our region early Sunday. Showers and a few \nthunderstorms will be likely ahead of the frontal boundary \nSunday...these within a typical summer humid airmass. A few \nstronger storms will be possible, especially along and south of \nthe NYS Thruway along with the potential both damaging wind \ngusts and hail as lapse rates steepen. PWats around 1.5 inches \nwill allow for a few drenching downpours of rain as well. Expect\nthe rain showers to end from west to east through \nmidday...though attention will quickly turn to a lake effect \nresponse. \n\nKey Message 2...Cool, upper level low to bring lake effect rain Sunday\nafternoon through Tuesday night. Cool Sunday night through\nMonday night.\n\nAn early taste of fall Sunday night through Monday night as a\ncool upper level low rotates across our region. Temperatures at\n850 hPa will plummet towards +7 to +8C across the eastern Great\nLakes. This will generate a lake effect rain response with the \ncool air flowing over the very warm lakes. Broken bands of lake \neffect rain will become disorganized through the day Monday with\ndaytime heating and the passage of a trough, but both Sunday \nnight a decent plume of lake effect rain, centered just near and\nsouth of Buffalo and Watertown, and Monday night into Tuesday \ncentered ESE of the Lakes is expected. This rain will diminish \nMonday night off Lake Erie, and Tuesday and Tuesday night off \nLake Ontario. In addition to the rain, there will be the \npotential for waterspouts along lake convergent boundaries with \ntall EL heights combined with the cool air associated with the \nupper level low over the warm lakes. \n\nOvernight lows will be some 5 to 8 degrees below normal Sunday night \nand Monday night, while high temperatures Monday will be some 10 to \n15 degrees below late August normals. \n\nKey Message 3...Showers and isolated thunderstorms Monday with the \npassage of Upper level trough axis. \n\nA surface trough, combined with an axis of an upper level low will \npass across the eastern Great Lakes region Monday. Instability is \nnot too impressive, generally under 300 J/KG of MUCAPE, but \nincreasing low level lapse rates of 6.5 to 7.0 C/km will aid in the \ngeneration of more widespread shower activity through the late \nmorning and early afternoon hours. Behind the initial cold front \nPWATs will not be as great, generally around an inch which will keep \nany heavier downpours limited, but the increasing lapse rates and \nlowering wet bulb zero heights could bring small hail within any \nstronger convective cell Monday.",
